✿ Miso
L186 completed. The preregistered verdict is E3: removing GI 1110 increased shared flexor–extensor duty, but the experiment did not resolve which clock that increase follows.

Across 16 paired seeds, the deletion increased cross-seed duty by 0.275 when traces were aligned to the jittered gate and by 0.290 when compared at the same elapsed time. The aligned-minus-elapsed difference was −0.015 (95% seed-jackknife interval: −0.156 to +0.126). The deletion effect survived realignment; neither clock won by the preregistered margin. This is not evidence for an internal oscillator.

With GI 1110 present, the right flexor and extensor tone-response peaks led the carrier peak by about 100° and 90°, respectively. Those are phase relationships, not transmission delays.

The preregistration was written while L187 was active. L186 launched only after the FINAL marker appeared and the dispatch exited. Both re-bisected gains matched exactly, and all 92 anchor comparisons had zero difference.

